---
import Layout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import MethodIllustration from '../../components/MethodIllustration.astro';
import PaperFigure from '../../components/PaperFigure.astro';
import TeachingFigure from '../../components/TeachingFigure.astro';
import PaperCitation from '../../components/PaperCitation.astro';
import TopicWorkbench from '../../components/TopicWorkbench';
import {citations} from '../../data/citations.mjs';
import {topics} from '../../data/research';
import '../../styles/method-pages.css';
export function getStaticPaths(){return topics.filter(topic=>topic.demo!=='docking').map(topic=>({params:{slug:topic.id},props:{topic}}));}
const {topic}=Astro.props,rep=topic.demo==='representations',screen=topic.demo==='screening',affinity=topic.demo==='binding-affinity';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const references=rep?[citations.unimolrep]:screen?[citations.fusion,citations.gradcam]:[citations.agima,citations.unimolrep,citations.gnina,citations.pdb1hsg,citations.pdb1hvr,citations.occlusion,citations.etdock];
const heading=rep?'Molecular representations':screen?'Molecular screening':'Bound-complex scoring';
const question=rep?'See how your molecule becomes a fingerprint, graph and spatial grid.':screen?'Get a relative screening score for your molecule and a protein target.':'Estimate affinity and pose quality for a bound protein–ligand complex.';
const takeaway=rep?'UniMolRep prepares complementary molecular views for prediction models; this tool does not predict a property.':screen?'DL-FSG combines molecular sequence and spatial shape. Compare scores for molecules against the same target.':'The live tool uses GNINA to score the bound pose you provide. Select the binding partners, read the affinity and pose scores, and explore the model’s spatial response.';
---
<Layout title={topic.title} description={topic.summary}>
<main id="main" class="wrap topic-document" data-method={topic.demo}>
<header class="topic-title"><a class="back-link" href={base+'#topics'}>← All four methods</a><div class="topic-opening"><div><p class="eyebrow">{rep?'01 / UniMolRep':screen?'02 / DL-FSG':'03 / GNINA — BAP'}</p><h1>{heading}</h1><p class="lead">{question}</p><p class="article-takeaway">{takeaway}</p><div class="article-actions"><a class="article-action-primary editorial-link" href="#experiment">Try your own input <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a href="#research">Explore the research <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></div><MethodIllustration kind={rep?'representations':screen?'screening':'binding-affinity'} eager/></div></header>
<div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span>On this page</span><a href="#experiment">Try the tool</a><a href="#concept">{affinity?'Reading the scores':'How it works'}</a>{affinity&&<a href="#occlusion">Spatial occlusion</a>}<a href="#research">{rep?'UniMolRep':screen?'DL-FSG':'AGIMA-Score'} findings</a>{screen&&<a href="#grad-cam">Reading Grad-CAM</a>}{affinity&&<a href="#etdock">Where poses come from</a>}<a href="#references">Papers & resources</a></aside>
<article class="article-content">
<section id="experiment" class="article-section experiment-section"><h2>{topic.analysisTitle}</h2><p class="tool-introduction">{rep?"Submit one SMILES. Explore the generated fingerprint, atom graph, angles, torsions and grid; download the data and conformer.":screen?"Submit one SMILES and choose a target. Calculate a relative score, explore its attribution and download the completed run.":"Load a PDB ID or upload a PDB complex. Select the protein chains and bound ligand, then score and download the supplied pose."}</p>
<TopicWorkbench initialTab={topic.demo} client:load/>
</section>
<section id="concept" class="article-section">
{rep?<>
<h2>Chemical identity is only part of the description</h2>
<p>Two representations of the same molecule need not give a model the same information. A fingerprint records chemical patterns. A graph records connections between atoms. Angles and torsions describe how connected atoms are arranged in space. UniMolRep brings these descriptions together so their effects can be studied systematically.</p>
<PaperFigure id="unimolrep-taxonomy"/>
<h3>From coordinates to a grid</h3><p>A molecular grid divides space into voxels. Separate channels record atomic features at each location, allowing a three-dimensional convolution to learn from local spatial neighbourhoods. The example below follows one aspirin conformer from its chemical structure to two element channels.</p>
<TeachingFigure kind="grid"/>
</>:screen?<>
<h2>One model per protein target</h2>
<p>Screening asks which compounds to prioritise from a collection. Each available target uses its own model, trained on compounds labelled for that protein. It scores a molecule using its SMILES and 3D shape. Visitors do not need to supply a protein sequence, structure or complex.</p>
<div class="figure-with-note"><PaperFigure id="fusion-screening-overview"/><div><h3>Why use two descriptions?</h3><p>A SMILES sequence records chemical connectivity, while a molecular grid provides spatial context. Our question was whether learning interactions between these descriptions improves the ranking of active compounds.</p></div></div>
</>:<>
<h2>Reading the scores for your supplied pose</h2>
<p>The live tool uses <a href={citations.gnina.url}>GNINA</a> to return two outputs. The <strong>affinity estimate</strong> is expressed in pK units: higher values indicate stronger predicted binding. The <strong>pose score</strong> ranges from 0 to 1 and reflects the model’s assessment of the supplied pose. It is not an affinity measurement.</p>
<h3 id="grid-scoring">How the complex becomes a model input</h3>
<p>Protein and ligand atoms occupy separate spatial channels in GNINA’s grid. The tool selects the chains and ligand you choose from a PDB structure, then scores that existing pose. It does not search for a new pose or measure affinity in an experiment.</p>
<TeachingFigure kind="scoring"/>
</>}
</section>
{affinity&&<section id="occlusion" class="article-section"><h2>What changes when a region is masked?</h2><p>Spatial occlusion examines how a prediction responds when part of the input grid is hidden. All channels within one region are set to zero, while the molecular coordinates stay fixed.</p><p>Evaluate the masked grid with the same model, then subtract its score from the original score. Positive differences mean that masking lowered the estimate; negative differences mean that it raised the estimate. The comparison measures the model’s sensitivity to each region.</p><TeachingFigure kind="occlusion"/></section>}
<section id="research" class="article-section research-section"><div class="research-resource-links"><a href={topic.paper.url}>Read the {rep?'UniMolRep':screen?'DL-FSG':'AGIMA-Score'} paper ↗</a>{affinity&&<a href={citations.gnina.url}>GNINA: the live scoring model ↗</a>}</div><h2>{rep?'UniMolRep: comparing representations':screen?'DL-FSG: learning across two molecular views':'AGIMA-Score: learning intermolecular adjacency'}</h2>
{rep?<>
<p>UniMolRep standardises preparation of fingerprints, graphs, geometric features, interaction features and grids. The toolkit covers small molecules, protein–ligand complexes and molecular trajectories. A shared preparation workflow makes it easier to compare representations for the same learning task.</p>
<PaperFigure id="unimolrep-workflow"/>
<h3>What the benchmarks show</h3><p>On Tox21, combining fingerprints with topological graphs improved classification performance. For QM9 regression, the strongest evaluated configuration included angle and torsion features. The results illustrate why a representation should be chosen for the prediction task.</p>
<PaperFigure id="unimolrep-small-molecule-benchmarks"/>
<h3 id="affinity-findings">The protein–ligand affinity comparison</h3><p>The PDBbind benchmark combines interaction fingerprints, graph features and geometric features. The combined 1D, 2D and 3D configuration achieved a Pearson correlation of <strong>0.7359</strong>, RMSE of <strong>1.3001</strong> and MAE of <strong>1.0132</strong>, leading the configurations evaluated in this study.</p>
<PaperFigure id="unimolrep-affinity-benchmarks"/>
<p>Our <a href={base+'topics/binding-affinity/#research'}>AGIMA-Score study</a> examines which protein–ligand contacts to encode when predicting affinity.</p>
</>:screen?<>
<p>DL-FSG encodes the SMILES sequence and ligand grid in parallel. Bidirectional cross-attention lets the features from each branch inform the other. The combined features then produce a target-specific screening score.</p>
<PaperFigure id="fusion-architecture"/>
<h3>Screening across 102 DUD-E targets</h3><p>The paper evaluates separately trained models across 102 DUD-E targets and reports aggregate AUROC <strong>0.9910</strong> and AUPRC <strong>0.9725</strong>. In the live tool, each available target has its own model. It scores a ligand from its SMILES and generated 3D shape; scores should be compared within one target.</p>
<PaperFigure id="fusion-performance"/>
<h3>What changes when a component is removed?</h3><p>Replacing cross-attention with self-attention reduced AUC to 0.9855. Using either SMILES or grids alone also reduced performance. The two ablation studies support learning from both representations and their interaction.</p><div class="ablation-figures"><PaperFigure id="fusion-attention-ablation"/><PaperFigure id="fusion-representation-ablation"/></div>
<p>Screening prioritises compounds for a target. Our <a href={base+'topics/protein-ligand-docking/'}>ETDock method</a> examines a related structural question: how a ligand might fit within its protein.</p>
</>:<>
<p>Binding affinity describes the strength of association between a ligand and its target protein. A binding structure records where the ligand sits within the protein. Our AGIMA-Score study asks which connections in that structure a model should learn from: every nearby atomic contact, or contacts across the binding interface?</p>
<div class="figure-with-note adjacency-note"><PaperFigure id="agima-adjacency"/><div><h3>Making the graph reflect the question</h3><p>In panel D, edges connect protein atoms to ligand atoms. Distance ranges distinguish different neighbourhoods. The graph therefore emphasises how the two binding partners meet.</p></div></div>
<div class="figure-with-note architecture-note"><PaperFigure id="agima-architecture"/><div><h3>From atomic contacts to a score</h3><p>AGIMA-Score combines atom features with distance-dependent, intermolecular adjacency matrices. Graph convolutions and aggregation produce features used to estimate binding strength.</p><p>The study examines alternative atom-feature sets and adjacency definitions. The default model uses two intermolecular contact ranges: 2–3 Å and 3–4 Å.</p></div></div>
<h3>Testing on independent complex sets</h3><p>Models were trained on PDBbind v2020 and tested on two CSAR sets. AGIMA-Score<sup>18</sup> achieved the lowest test RMSEs in the comparison: <strong>1.5806</strong> and <strong>1.6279</strong>. The eight-feature variant achieved the highest Test1 correlation, <strong>0.7414</strong>.</p>
<PaperFigure id="agima-scoring-benchmark"/>
<h3>Investigating what the model uses</h3><p>Feature masking measures how validation performance changes when one feature type is removed. In the eight-feature variant, excluded volume has the largest effect.</p><PaperFigure id="agima-feature-importance"/>
<p>UniMolRep addresses the complementary question of how representation combinations affect affinity prediction. See its <a href={base+'topics/representing-molecules/#affinity-findings'}>PDBbind comparison</a> for results using fingerprints, graphs and geometric features together.</p>
</>}
</section>
{screen&&<section id="grad-cam" class="article-section"><h2>Reading the grid branch with Grad-CAM</h2><p>Grad-CAM highlights spatial regions associated with a higher screening score. It uses score gradients to weight the grid branch’s feature maps. The gradients pass through the sequence–grid fusion model while the molecule and conformer remain fixed.</p><TeachingFigure kind="cam"/><p>Each voxel summarises a spatial neighbourhood, giving a coarse view of the model’s response. Read the map at this scale rather than as a contribution from an individual atom. Colours are scaled to the peak of this prediction. The map shows model attribution, not proof of a binding mechanism; colour strength should not be compared across different runs.</p></section>}
{affinity&&
<section id="etdock" class="article-section research-section" aria-labelledby="etdock-heading">
<h2 id="etdock-heading">Where does a binding pose come from?</h2>
<p>Scoring starts with an arrangement of protein and ligand atoms. Docking addresses the preceding structural question: how might a ligand fit its protein? ETDock learns from connectivity and geometry to predict a three-dimensional binding pose.</p>
<a class="editorial-link" href={base+'topics/protein-ligand-docking/'}>Explore ETDock and binding-pose prediction <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
</section>
}

<section id="references" class="article-section references"><div class="source-details"><h2>Papers & resources</h2>{affinity&&<p>AGIMA-Score resources: <a href="https://github.com/debbydanwang/DL-PLBAP">research code</a> and <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/15023336">trained model and data</a>.</p>}<ol>{references.map((paper,i)=><li id={'ref-'+(i+1)}><PaperCitation paper={paper}/></li>)}</ol></div></section>
<a class="back-link" href={base+'#topics'}>← All four methods</a>
</article></div></main></Layout>
